## Room for two

The same fifteen square metres as the Twin, laid out instead around one double bed. The headboard wall is clad floor to ceiling in pale timber, with the wardrobe built into the same run so it reads as part of the architecture rather than a cupboard pushed against a wall.

Behind the bed, a panel of geometric wallpaper in sage green picks up the colour of the curtains at the two tall windows. Those windows are the making of the room: they run almost the full height of the wall and look straight out into the plane trees along Ceintuurbaan, which in summer fill the whole frame in green. A black metal console sits beneath the television with the coffee machine, the kettle and cups on it, and a low seat by the window is the spot you will end up in.

The bathroom has a corner shower behind curved glass, a wall-hung basin, a heated towel rail and a mirror lit from above. Everything is white-tiled and easy to keep in order.

It is a straightforward base for a few days in the city, and being one floor up the street noise is more suggestion than sound. Breakfast is served at the front of the hotel from 07:30 to 10:30, and there are drinks and snacks on the house in the lobby from the afternoon into the evening.

Free WiFi, a flat-screen TV, and tea and coffee whenever you want them.
